;; Regulatory source register for JPN-METI export control under the Foreign
;; Exchange and Foreign Trade Act (外為法) and METI industrial-policy
;; subsidy/grant programs.
;;
;; README.md says: "Every regulatory claim must cite the official METI source."
;; Until this file existed there was no such list, so no claim in this
;; repository could be traced to anything. This is that list.
;;
;; Shape is tx-data (a vector of entity maps), like every other EDN corpus in
;; this workspace, so it loads with
;;   (d/transact conn (edn/read-string (slurp "facts.edn")))
;; :source/id is the join key. A law or page not in this table has NO
;; spec-basis for this repository -- extend the table, never invent an id or a
;; URL.
;;
;; VERIFIED BY `nbb scripts/verify-facts.cljs`, which re-fetches every entry.
;; Each carries a :source/verify tag naming WHICH check establishes it.
;;
;;   :e-gov-law-id   Resolve the law id through the e-Gov law API and require
;;                   the title and the law number to still match. HTTP status
;;                   is never consulted here and must not be:
;;                   laws.e-gov.go.jp is a single-page app that answers
;;                   HTTP 200 for /law/<anything>, renders "not found"
;;                   client-side, and therefore cannot tell a real citation
;;                   from an invented one.
;;
;;   :page-identity  Fetch and require four things together: a 2xx, a final
;;                   URL that is still the page asked for, the declared
;;                   charset, and the <title> recorded below.
;;
;;   :page-text      All of :page-identity, plus every string in
;;                   :page/must-contain present in the de-tagged body.
;;
;; THE MINISTRY'S OWN SITE IS BEHIND A BOT CHALLENGE, AND THE CHALLENGE IS A
;; 2xx. THIS IS THE CENTRAL MEASUREMENT OF THIS REGISTER.
;;
;; www.meti.go.jp sits behind AWS WAF. To an automated client it serves, in
;; place of the page asked for, a 2468-byte JavaScript challenge carrying
;; window.awsWafCookieDomainList and window.gokuProps. That response:
;;
;;   * answers HTTP **202**, which is a 2xx, so a status-only checker reports
;;     a pass for a page it never read;
;;   * is served AT THE REQUESTED URL with no redirect, so comparing the final
;;     URL does not catch it either;
;;   * carries an EMPTY <title>, which is the only field that separates it
;;     from the authority.
;;
;; A verifier that checks the title but classifies the mismatch as ordinary
;; title drift reports "the register is wrong" for a run that never reached
;; the authority at all. That is the failure this workspace keeps finding in
;; other forms: a check that could not run returning the same value as a check
;; that ran and found nothing. The verifier therefore detects the challenge
;; explicitly and REFUSES -- exit 2, neither pass nor register error.
;;
;; Measured 2026-08-26: a cold client gets real pages; roughly ten requests
;; trips the challenge, which then holds for about four and a half minutes,
;; and single requests spaced fourteen seconds apart re-trip it. Because the
;; block is not reliably avoidable from here, THIS REGISTER CITES NO PAGE ON
;; www.meti.go.jp. Its host entity is kept to hold the measurement and to give
;; the challenge detector a live host, exactly as the sibling MLIT register
;; keeps its soft-404 bureau host without citing a page on it.
;;
;; That is a limit on this register, not a claim about METI. The pages exist
;; and a browser reads them. They are not-cited, not absent, and
;; :coverage/not-covered says so.
;;
;; THE PAGES THAT ARE CITED ARE ON THE THREE AGENCY HOSTS THAT ANSWER.
;; 特許庁, 中小企業庁 and 資源エネルギー庁 are METI extra-ministerial bureaux
;; on their own hosts, and none of the three is challenged.
;;
;; THE HOST ASSUMPTIONS ARE MEASURED AT RUN TIME, NOT DATED IN A COMMENT.
;; The :host-behaviour entities below say what each host does with a path that
;; does not exist. The verifier fetches a nonexistent path on every host in the
;; register and fails if the answer has changed. A dated note saying "measured
;; to refuse correctly" degrades into a constant the moment it is written; a
;; declaration the run re-measures cannot.
;;
;; ALL FOUR HOSTS ANSWER **403**, NOT 404, FOR A PATH THAT DOES NOT EXIST.
;; Each serves a real Japanese "this page does not exist" body at the requested
;; URL, with its own byte count and its own title. 403 is not 2xx, so status
;; still discriminates -- but a checker written to look for 404 specifically,
;; or one that reads 403 as "blocked, cannot measure", would misreport four
;; hosts that answered perfectly clearly. The status each host actually
;; returned is pinned in :host/missing-status and re-measured.
;;
;; :source/covers says why an entry is in an export-control and
;; industrial-policy register. Claims stop at the level actually checked --
;; law identity, and for the agency pages the title, the URL, the charset and
;; the strings read on 2026-08-26. No article text and no PDF is asserted,
;; because none was read.

[
 ;; ── What each host does with a path that does not exist ────────────────────
 ;; Re-measured on every run. A change here invalidates the check that rests
 ;; on it, which is the point of writing it down as data.

 {:source/id "host.www-meti-go-jp"
  :source/kind :host-behaviour
  :host/name "www.meti.go.jp"
  :host/missing-path :refuses-or-challenges
  :host/missing-status 403
  :host/challenge :aws-waf
  :source/note "経済産業省 itself. Two behaviours, and the register has to allow
                both because which one appears depends on how recently this
                client called. Cold, a nonexistent path answers 403 with a
                5045-byte 指定されたページまたはファイルは存在しません page.
                Under the AWS WAF challenge, the same request answers 202 with
                an empty title. Only a real 2xx page body -- a genuine soft 404
                -- is a failure here. No page on this host is cited, so nothing
                in the register rests on reaching it; this entity exists to
                record the measurement and to give the challenge detector a
                live host."}

 {:source/id "host.www-jpo-go-jp"
  :source/kind :host-behaviour
  :host/name "www.jpo.go.jp"
  :host/missing-path :refuses-or-challenges
  :host/missing-status 403
  :host/challenge :aws-waf
  :source/note "特許庁. Cold, a nonexistent path answers 403 with a 9939-byte
                お探しのページが見つかりません page served at the requested URL,
                and the title of that page differs from the front page title, so
                both status and title separate a real page from an invented one.

                It is ALSO behind the same AWS WAF as the ministry host, which
                is not obvious from casual use: it served hundreds of bytes of
                real pages during this register's construction and only
                challenged when asked for the SAME page five times in a row
                about a second and a half apart. What produced that burst was
                this register's own verifier -- an early revision drove every
                page self-test against one probe entry. The verifier refused,
                correctly, and reported nothing about the register; the fix was
                to spread the self-tests across hosts, and to put a host
                measured to challenge LAST in the self-test pool rather than
                first.

                The block here did not clear the way the ministry host's did.
                www.meti.go.jp answered normally again about four and a half
                minutes after being tripped; this host was still challenging
                the better part of an hour later, under repeated checking that
                may itself have kept renewing it. So: the recovery time is not
                a property of AWS WAF that can be read off one host and applied
                to another, and no number for it is written down here as though
                it were.

                So a challenge here is a declared behaviour, not a failure. Only
                a real 2xx page body for a path that does not exist is."}

 {:source/id "host.www-chusho-meti-go-jp"
  :source/kind :host-behaviour
  :host/name "www.chusho.meti.go.jp"
  :host/missing-path :refuses
  :host/missing-status 403
  :source/note "中小企業庁. Answers 403 with a 2950-byte
                指定されたページまたはファイルは存在しません page -- the same
                wording METI's own host uses, on a different host with a
                different byte count and no ministry suffix in the title."}

 {:source/id "host.www-enecho-meti-go-jp"
  :source/kind :host-behaviour
  :host/name "www.enecho.meti.go.jp"
  :host/missing-path :refuses
  :host/missing-status 403
  :source/note "資源エネルギー庁. Answers 403 with a 34244-byte
                お探しのページが見つかりません page -- larger than most real
                pages on the host, because it carries the full site navigation.
                Body size is not a signal; the status is."}

